    {
      "url": "https://www.lawlessgerman.com/grammar/possessive-adjectives/",
      "title": "German Possessive Adjectives",
      "description": "A clear reference on mein, dein, sein and the rest of the possessive adjectives, with example sentences and the case endings each one takes.",
      "howToUse": "Skim it once for the pattern, then say five sentences about your own family out loud using mein/dein before checking the endings table.",
      "summary": "Lawless German grammar page covering the full set of German possessive adjectives (mein, dein, sein, ihr, unser, euer, Ihr) and how their endings change with case and gender. Includes example sentences showing agreement with the noun they modify. Useful as a reference for a learner building sentences about family members (mein Vater, meine Schwester, unsere Eltern) and needing to see the case-ending pattern spelled out rather than just drilled.",
      "goalTitles": ["Talk about your family"]
    },
    {
      "url": "https://www.germanveryeasy.com/perfect-tense",
      "title": "Perfect Tense (Perfekt)",
      "description": "A short explainer on how to build the Perfekt with haben or sein plus a past participle, including the common irregular participles.",
      "howToUse": "Read it once, then retell your last weekend in three or four Perfekt sentences before your next session, out loud or written.",
      "summary": "GermanVeryEasy page explaining the formation of the Perfekt (present perfect used for past events in spoken German): choice of haben vs sein as auxiliary, participle formation for weak and strong verbs, and word order (auxiliary in second position, participle at the end). Directly supports building sentences like 'Ich habe... gemacht' or 'Ich bin... gegangen' for weekend small talk, and touches on the verb-second/participle-final word order pattern relevant to German sentence structure more broadly.",
      "goalTitles": ["Handle small talk about your weekend"]
    }
  ]
}
